Status Epilepticus
Acute Urgent Care
Continuous seizures >5 minutes, Incomplete recovery between seizures, Unremitting epileptiform discharges on EEG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-047
Reason for Visit: Unstoppable, violent, full-body convulsions lasting over 5 minutes without the patient waking up.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who collapsed and has been violently convulsing continuously for more than 5 minutes. This defines status epilepticus, a profound neurological emergency. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment and administration of a rescue benzodiazepine were mandated to abort the seizure, as prolonged convulsions lead to irreversible hypoxic brain injury, severe lactic acidosis, and hyperthermic neuronal death. This situation cannot wait for standard ambulance or clinic response times.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Spouse reports] He fell to the floor and started shaking violently 10 minutes ago, and he hasn't stopped or opened his eyes once!
History of Present Illness: The patient, who has a known history of epilepsy, presents with continuous, unremitting generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity. The spouse reports the convulsive event began over 10 minutes ago and has not ceased. There is a profound loss of consciousness and a total lack of responsiveness. The spouse notes rhythmic, violent muscle contractions and rigidity alternating with relaxation phases. There are signs of respiratory compromise, including cyanosis around the lips and irregular, stertorous breathing. The patient has been incontinent of urine during the episode.
Functional Status: Unconscious, actively convulsing on the living room floor.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of fatal hypoxic brain injury and aspiration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Unobtainable during seizure | HR: 140 | Temp: 38.5 | RR: Irregular/Shallow | SpO2: 88% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is actively convulsing (generalized tonic-clonic), diaphoretic, cyanotic, and unresponsive.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is limited by active convulsions but confirms continuous generalized tonic-clonic motor activity. The patient's jaw is clenched tightly. Pupils are dilated and unreactive. Respiratory examination reveals significant compromise; the patient is hypoxic (SpO2 88%) due to prolonged muscular contractions of the chest wall and excessive oral secretions blocking the airway. Cardiovascular exam shows severe tachycardia. The patient is hyperthermic (38.5°C) secondary to extreme sustained muscular exertion. Expected investigations in the ED would include an arterial blood gas revealing severe lactic acidosis and hypoxia, and a continuous electroencephalogram (cEEG) demonstrating unremitting generalized epileptiform discharges.
Environmental Observations: Furniture has been pushed away by the spouse; patient is currently safe from secondary physical trauma.
Medication Reconciliation: Spouse reports the patient ran out of their regular Levetiracetam three days ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Status Epilepticus - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pseudoseizure (Psychogenic Non-Epileptic Seizure) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of objective physiological compromise (severe cyanosis, hypoxia, hyperthermia, tachycardia, incontinence), which are hallmarks of true massive electrical discharge rather than a psychogenic event.

Cardiac Arrest (Hypoxic Convulsions) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a strong, rapid central pulse.

Eclampsia - Ruled out/unlikely due to male gender; in females, this must always be considered if pregnant or recently postpartum.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the absolute clinical definition of generalized convulsive status epilepticus: continuous seizure activity lasting longer than 5 minutes without recovery of consciousness. The severe hypoxia, hyperthermia, and tachycardia indicate profound physiological exhaustion that rapidly leads to permanent neuronal necrosis and cardiovascular collapse. My immediate physical presence allowed for the administration of potent mucosal/parenteral benzodiazepines to chemically abort the seizure, followed immediately by airway positioning to reverse the critical hypoxia. Delaying this intervention would have resulted in certain, devastating brain injury.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately positioned patient in the lateral recovery position to protect the airway and facilitate drainage of secretions. Administered Midazolam 10mg Intramuscularly (IM) into the lateral thigh. Upgraded 000 call to Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch for advanced airway management. Seizure activity ceased 3 minutes post-administration. Patient remained in a profound post-ictal coma (GCS 3). Maintained airway via head-tilt/chin-lift and applied high-flow oxygen.
Positive Behavioral Change: By never abruptly stopping your anti-seizure medication, even if you run out of repeats, you prevent your brain from experiencing massive, uncontrolled electrical storms like this one.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport to the Emergency Department.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; requires urgent neurological review and IV anticonvulsant loading.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The spouse is instructed to keep the patient strictly in the lateral recovery position. If the patient stops breathing entirely, roll them flat and commence CPR. If convulsions restart before paramedics arrive, a second dose of IM Midazolam may be required.

